## Flutter's Preferred State Tool
Just like React Native needs Zustand or Redux, Flutter needs global state management. While many exist (Bloc, Riverpod), **Provider** is the official recommendation for beginners.

Provider acts as a dependency injection system. You create a class that holds your data and extends `ChangeNotifier`. 

```dart
class CartModel extends ChangeNotifier {
  int items = 0;
  void add() {
    items++;
    notifyListeners(); // Tells the UI to update
  }
}
```
You wrap your app in a `ChangeNotifierProvider`. Deep down in the widget tree, any widget can use `context.watch<CartModel>()` to read the data. When `notifyListeners()` is called, only the specific widgets watching that model are efficiently rebuilt.

## The Navigator 2.0
Routing in Flutter is managed by the `Navigator` widget, which manages a stack of Route objects.

For simple apps, you can use **Imperative Routing**:
```dart
// Push a new screen on top of the stack
Navigator.push(context, MaterialPageRoute(builder: (context) => DetailsScreen()));

// Pop the current screen off the stack (go back)
Navigator.pop(context);
```
However, for complex apps (especially Flutter Web apps where you need the browser back button to work perfectly), you use the more complex **Navigator 2.0 (Declarative Routing)** via packages like `go_router`. This defines routes as a strict tree of URLs, making deep-linking (opening the app directly to a specific product page from an email link) incredibly robust.

## http and json_serializable
To fetch data from a REST API in Flutter, you typically use the official `http` package.

Because Dart is strongly typed, you cannot just parse JSON into a random JavaScript object. You must explicitly define a Dart class that mirrors the JSON structure.

```dart
final response = await http.get(Uri.parse('https://api.com/user'));
if (response.statusCode == 200) {
  // Decode JSON string to Map, then to strong Dart object
  var data = jsonDecode(response.body);
  return User.fromJson(data);
}
```
Writing the `fromJson` methods by hand is tedious. Most professional Flutter developers use code generation tools like `json_serializable` or `freezed`, which automatically write all the JSON parsing code for you, ensuring type safety without the boilerplate.
